he definition of  what a ghost is is of course pretty hard  to pin down and I don't think we should  get caught up in linguistics in this  video so we'll just consider ghosts to  be you know ghosts we all know what  ghosts is is it's the it's the spirit of  a dead person that comes back and haunts  the living this of course directly  relates to our ideas about the ass  life if ghosts are indeed real then  there must be an afterlife and if you  believe in ghosts then on some level at  least you must believe in a life after  this one  ghosts are sometimes evil and sometimes  not the most important part is that  they're dead and that there's still  somehow around and let's be honest in  almost every case they are evil or at  the very least scary Japan is famous for  its horror films that were especially  popular around the turn of the 21st  century and these include films like the  ring and you on The Grudge and and and  many more but stories like these aren't  new to Japanese culture in fact Japan  has a very long history of telling ghost  stories called Cayden and many of these  films are taken directly or at least  inspired by many of these storytelling  traditions much like our European  conception of ghosts the Japanese  equivalent which is often called au ray  and please excuse all pronunciations  from now on is the spirit of a dead  person that has been kept from a  peaceful afterlife  they are stuck in a kind of purgatory in  between our world and the next and is  thus able to haunt the living the  funeral is very important for the  well-being of a spirit after death and  the way in which the person died also  plays a major role if everything goes  well including the funeral then the  spirit can move on to the afterlife and  and sort of be at peace however if the  person died in a very violent way like  if he was or she was murdered or  committed suicide then something else  happens the spirit will then often  become a you day a kind of ghost driven  by emotions of hatred and revenge as a  result of the way in which they died  they can then enter our world and enact  that revenge on the person who killed  them or just on anyone that happens to  come in their way which is actually  often the case in these stories for  those of you who have seen some of these  Japanese horror films these themes will  seem very familiar  to you in the ring for example the ghost  of a dead girl who was murdered by being  tossed into a well haunts and kills  anyone who watches a specific videotape  the way in which she died made her a  vengeful spirit that seeks to enact  revenge on the living  similarly in jus on The Grudge the ghost  of a mother and her child who were  brutally murdered in their house haunts  anyone who enters said house they are  both very frightening and dark stories  that take heavy inspiration from the  traditional ghost stories of Japan in  many of these cases the spirit or ghost  can be freed or released by helping it  achieve its purpose by for example  enacting revenge on those who kill them  but in many cases even this doesn't help  another significant and interesting  detail is the appearance in old  theatrical performances of ghost stories  in Japan the ghost or Ouray were  eventually given a very characteristic  look to emphasize that they were ghosts  one they always had long unkept black  hair and two they were often dressed in  white sort of funeral or burial clothing  again we can here see clear influences  on modern Japanese horror films the  image of a woman dressed in white with  long black hair has become  characteristic of that kind of film in  one of the more famous quedan or ghost  stories from Japanese tradition called  the dish mansion at Bonn show it tells  of a servant girl called o'kiku who  works for a samurai the samurai falls in  love with her and at multiple location  makes advances towards her but she  always refuses he thing comes up with a  plan and makes it seem like the servant  has lost 