und the turn of the last century and  in a part in Western Sweden where I  we grew up it was a winter's day and my  great-great grandfather or something  like that was on his way home from work  but as he was walking along one of the  roads he saw his wife out in the field  and she was jumping around in a strange  way from left to right and he was very  confused I thought this was strange so  he called out to her but she wouldn't  answer him she just kept jumping and  eventually she disappeared behind the  treeline and he of course still thought  this was very strange but he kind of  shook it off and kept walking home and  as he came home he told his friends and  family who were there what he had seen  that he had seen his wife jumping around  in this field and he asked them if they  knew what she was doing why didn't why  she was out there and the people there  were shocked and they told him don't you  know your wife died earlier today in a  drowning accident ghost stories like  these are apparent all over folklore all  around the world there is something  about our fascination with death and the  afterlife that compels us to wonder and  sometimes shiver at the possibilities  this theme will be continued in the next  episode when I'll be talking about the  long-awaited holiday of Halloween as  well as some of our most beloved pop  culture monsters I'll see you then
 

